  "marvel-snap": {
    cost_per_pull: null,
    cost_to_pity: null,
    pulls_to_pity: null,
    has_pity_system: false,
    currency_name: "Gold / Credits",
    currency_per_dollar: null,
    score_transparency: 9,
    score_value: 8,
    score_fairness: 9,
    score_player_control: 9,
    comparable_slugs: ["magic-the-gathering-arena", "hearthstone"],
    overview_html: `<p><strong>Marvel Snap</strong> is a collectible card game developed by Second Dinner featuring fast-paced card battles with Marvel characters. The game employs a non-traditional gacha model: instead of gacha pulls, card acquisition occurs through Collection Level progression. Players accumulate Credits (earned from matches, daily rewards, and events) and unlock new cards automatically as their Collection Level increases. The premium monetism currency (Credits) is earned entirely through gameplay—cards are never directly purchased, and purchasing Credits only accelerates the natural progression rate.</p><p>Marvel Snap's monetization philosophy diverges fundamentally from traditional gachas. There is no gacha RNG, no pity systems, and no pay-to-unlock-cards mechanics. Instead, the game monetizes through cosmetics (card variants, borders), Spotlight Caches (accelerated card access), and season pass ($9.99 premium battle pass) rewards. This design eliminates predatory gatekeeping around competitive functionality while capturing revenue through cosmetics and convenience. The result is a card game where competitive deck construction requires only time engagement, not spending.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>Marvel Snap has no gacha mechanics or pity systems because it doesn't use gacha for card acquisition.</strong> Cards are acquired through Collection Level progression—as you accumulate Credits from matches and events, your Collection Level increases automatically. Each Collection Level increase unlocks a new card, with the unlock sequence predetermined and published. There is no RNG, no gambling, and no uncertainty in card acquisition.</p><p>This system is exceptionally transparent (9/10) because there's nothing hidden. Every card's unlock location and requirement is publicly available. Players know exactly how many matches/Credits are required to access any specific card. The absence of pity is irrelevant because there's no gatekeeping—every card is guaranteed through engagement, not chance.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2021:</strong> Marvel Snap launched in closed beta with a revolutionary non-gacha monetization model. Second Dinner explicitly rejected traditional CCG gacha mechanics in favor of engagement-based card access. This design philosophy generated industry attention as proof that card games could thrive without predatory acquisition mechanics.</p><p><strong>2022:</strong> The game's open launch maintained the non-gacha philosophy while introducing cosmetic monetization through card variants and borders. Revenue streams came from cosmetics (no gameplay impact) and season pass ($9.99), demonstrating viable alternative to card-gacha models. The competitive community embraced the fairness—deck construction required only time engagement.</p><p><strong>2023-2024:</strong> Marvel Snap continued success with regular set releases and card additions. The game evolved cosmetics monetization while maintaining zero pay-for-functionality design. Annual revenues exceeded $100M, proving non-gacha card games remain profitable within CCG market.</p><p><strong>2024-Present:</strong> Marvel Snap serves as industry case study for non-predatory card game design. Discussions about Magic: The Gathering Arena and Hearthstone monetization ethics frequently reference Marvel Snap as proof that fairness doesn't eliminate profitability.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>Marvel Snap has experienced minimal controversy due to its non-predatory design. The primary discussion (not controversy) centered on whether cosmetic-only monetization remained sustainable for live-service games. Second Dinner's consistent profitability answered this affirmatively.</p><p>A minor discussion emerged around regional content availability when certain Marvel licenses didn't extend globally, restricting some cards in specific regions. However, this reflected licensing complexities rather than monetization ethics.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>1. Focus on daily engagement for free card acquisition:</strong> Cards unlock through Collection Level progression, which increases from playing matches and completing daily quests. Consistent engagement—even casual 2-3 match sessions daily—generates reliable card acquisition without spending.</p><p><strong>2. Prioritize collection completion naturally without rushing:</strong> The game's entire card library is guaranteed through time. Resist temptation to purchase Credits to accelerate card access—the natural progression pace is sustainable and competitive decks are buildable at any engagement level.</p><p><strong>3. Monetize only on cosmetics if desired:</strong> Card variants and borders are entirely cosmetic with zero gameplay impact. If you support the game financially, cosmetic purchases are the ethical choice versus progression acceleration.</p><p><strong>4. Utilize event rewards for accelerated Credit accumulation:</strong> Limited-time events provide bonus Credits beyond typical match rewards. Participating in events during their windows accelerates Collection Level progression moderately.</p><p><strong>5. Leverage the predetermined unlock sequence for deck planning:</strong> Every card's unlock position is published. Use this information to plan which cards you'll access soonest, allowing optimal deck construction with forthcoming cards.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>Marvel Snap is the ethical gold standard for card game monetization. The absence of gacha mechanics isn't a limitation—it's deliberate design prioritizing fairness. The transparency score of 9/10 is nearly perfect because there's nothing to hide: card acquisition sequence is published, costs are clear, and progression is guaranteed through engagement. The fairness score of 9/10 reflects that competitive functionality is entirely free—no pay-to-win mechanics exist.</p><p>The value proposition is exceptional precisely because there's no spending pressure. F2P players access competitive decks through consistent daily engagement. Casual spenders purchase cosmetics or season pass without affecting competitiveness. Whales purchase cosmetics exclusively—no spending accelerates gameplay functionality. This eliminates the spending tiers that plague traditional gachas, creating perfect horizontal monetization.</p><p>Marvel Snap demonstrates that card games can be profitable without predatory mechanics. The game earns $100M+ annually through cosmetics alone, disproving industry claims that gatekeeping cards is necessary for revenue. Second Dinner's success influences ongoing CCG monetization discussions and provides reference point for "how not to be exploitative."</p><p>Recommendation: Marvel Snap is unequivocally recommended for all players. The complete absence of pay-to-win mechanics makes it one of the fairest competitive games available on any platform. F2P players should play freely and engage with the game on their own schedule—competitive decks are entirely achievable through time engagement. There is no spending pressure whatsoever. This is the model for ethical gacha and card game design that the industry should emulate.</p>`,
  },

